// =============================================================================
// MemQL Logic Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `logic` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it -- see core/dslfs/walker.go). Two tests parse it:
// component/memql/reference_skeletons_test.go and
// component/memql/sense/reference_diagnose_test.go.
//
// `logic` is the behavioral DSL's PURE-DECISION construct. The one-line
// mental model from the behavioral-constructs ADR
// (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md, §2):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// A logic reads via queries, composes and decides, and returns a value. It is
// deterministic and unit-testable by construction: the same inputs always
// produce the same output, with no observable side effect. That purity is what
// lets a logic be replayed and fingerprint-verified and reused from any
// context.
//
// A logic's body is the statement language every logic and automation is
// written in (docs/public/language/memql.md#bodies): statements that run in
// the order they are written, the last of them a `return`.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call-graph contract (ADR §2 table)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Construct | May call                                  | May NOT
//   ----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------
//   logic     | queries, other logic, read-only builtins  | mutations, actions,
//             |                                           | publish, automations,
//             |                                           | triggers, side effects
//
// Two gates hold it. The body language refuses `publish`, an `automation` call
// and an `action` call in a logic at load (body_publish_in_logic,
// body_call_not_in_logic). The call-graph contract (component/memql/callgraph,
// run over the tree by test/dslconformance and over every authored construct
// by the authoring sandbox) also refuses a `mutation` call and a
// side-effecting builtin (logic-purity, read-only-builtin).
//
// Concretely, inside a logic you MAY:
//   - call a `query` (read the graph),
//   - call another `logic`,
//   - call a READ-ONLY `builtin` (an `@executor("integration.*")` whose
//     capability `sideEffectClass` is `read` -- e.g. serviceVersion),
//   - compose and decide: `if` / `else`, `for x in <source>`, `switch`,
//     `p ? a : b`, `??`, arithmetic, and the collection library (section 6).
//
// Inside a logic you MAY NOT:
//   - call a `mutation` (graph write)         -> belongs in an automation,
//   - call an `action` (external side effect)  -> belongs in an automation,
//   - `publish` an event                       -> belongs in an automation,
//   - call a side-effecting builtin            -> reclassify it as an action,
//   - carry a `@trigger`                        -> only automations are reactive.
//
// The single-writer instinct is preserved but RELOCATED to the automation
// (decide via logic -> persist via one mutation). See ADR §2.1.

use cluster.queries.{ existingCluster }
use common.builtins.{ serviceVersion }

// =============================================================================
//   1. Logic-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `logic` keyword.
//
//   /// text               The description: a doc comment above the
//                          declaration (@description("text") is the same).
//   @disabled              Skip the logic at load. A construct is enabled by
//                          default; @enabled was the explicit-on no-op and is
//                          RETIRED (epic memql#5375).
//   @actor                 The body reads actor.* (required when it does).
//   @requiresRank("<role>")
//   @requiresCapability("<verb>", "<resource>")
//                          Who may CALL the logic, enforced at execution and
//                          validated at load.
//   @eventField("a", "b")  On an event-fed logic: the payload fields
//                          args.event.payload.<field> may read.
//
// A logic is never a standalone entry point. To make one reachable from
// outside the DSL, expose it through a triggered `automation` or a `tool` with
// @handler(type="function", name="theLogic"). Every logic must be referenced
// by some logic / query / tool / automation.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Anatomy -- args, statements, return
// =============================================================================
//
// A logic declares its inputs in an `args { ... }` block, and its statements
// follow it directly, the last of them `return <expr>`, which is the logic's
// value.
//
// Args field syntax mirrors every other construct:
//   <name> <type>[!] [@enum("a","b")] [@maxLength(N)] [@pattern("re")]
//                     [@minimum(N)] [@maximum(N)]
// `!` (or @required) makes a field required; a field is described by a ///
// doc comment above it. Read caller args as `args.X` -- a bare `X` is refused
// (body_unknown_name). Engine values are bare roots: `now`, `actor.X`,
// `config.X`, `partition`. A logic has no trigger of its own: an event-fed
// logic declares `event object!` and reads `args.event.payload.<field>`.

/// Pure gate check: was the deploy gate green? Defaults to false when the gate
/// result is absent.
logic deployGatePassed {
  args {
    gate object!
  }
  return args.gate.passed ?? false
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. One statement -- the common case
// =============================================================================
//
// When the decision is one expression, the logic is a single `return`. A logic
// may return a constant -- e.g. the canonical engine node-type set the
// deployment bundle iterates over. Engine mesh ONLY (no bff / product nodes).

/// The engine cluster node types the deployment bundle manages.
logic engineNodeTypes {
  return ["identity", "bff", "agent", "planner", "workbench", "mcp", "edge"]
}

// A value that depends on a condition is `p ? a : b`, or a name each branch of
// an if binds (a branch runs at most once and shares the scope around it):

/// Pick the k8s overlay path for a deploy target.
logic overlayPathForProvider {
  args {
    provider enum("docker-local", "azure")!
  }
  if args.provider == "docker-local" {
    path := "deploy/k8s/overlays/local"
  } else {
    path := "deploy/k8s/overlays/cloud"
  }
  return path
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Several statements -- read, compose, decide
// =============================================================================
//
// `name := <call>` binds a call's value to `name` from the next statement on;
// every call names its kind and passes named arguments, and is a statement of
// its own. Every call here is a READ (a query or a read-only builtin) or pure
// composition -- never a mutation or action.

/// Decide whether the cluster still needs bootstrapping, and at what version.
logic bootstrapDecision {
  args {
    event object!
  }
  existing := query existingCluster()
  version := builtin serviceVersion()
  needsBootstrap := existing.empty() && args.event.payload.node.type == "bff"
  return { needsBootstrap: needsBootstrap, version: version }
}

// The automation that consumes this decision is where the WRITE happens:
//
//   decide := logic bootstrapDecision(event: event)
//   if decide.needsBootstrap {
//     mutation createCluster(version: decide.version)   // one mutation, one write
//   }
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. NON-COMPLIANT examples
// =============================================================================
//
// 5a. A logic that performs a write. Refused by the call-graph contract
//     (logic-purity): relocate the write to an automation.
//
//   logic registerNodeBad {
//     args {
//       event object!
//     }
//     node := mutation createNode(id: args.event.payload.node.id)  // <- WRITE
//     return node
//   }
//
// 5b. A logic that reaches the outside world, or publishes. Refused at load
//     (body_call_not_in_logic, body_publish_in_logic).
//
//   logic deployBad {
//     args {
//       ref string!
//     }
//     return action cloneRepoAtVersion(ref: args.ref)   // <- refused
//   }
//
// 5c. A logic that carries a trigger. Reactivity is automations-only.
//
//   @trigger(event="system.startup")   // <- refused on a logic
//   logic onStartupBad {
//     return true
//   }
//
// 5d. The retired body forms, each refused at parse by name and rewritten by
//     `memqlmigrate --rewrite=bodies`: a `body { ... }` wrapper around the
//     statements (body_block_retired); `x := if <cond> { <call> }`
//     (body_conditional_assign_retired -- bind the name inside the branch);
//     `for x := range <source>` (body_for_range_retired -- `for x in`);
//     `steps.<name>.result` (body_steps_reference_retired -- read `<name>`);
//     a call with no kind (body_call_kind_missing); and an argument without a
//     name (body_positional_argument).
//
// =============================================================================
//   6. Collection / lambda library (ADR §2.2, #2302)
// =============================================================================
//
// A single method-chained collection library with arrow lambdas is the
// in-memory, post-fetch way to filter / project / aggregate a list inside a
// logic (and in an automation's `for` source). In a spec or a query filter
// only `any`, `all` and `count()` over a row's array field push down; every
// other method there takes only a list that does not read the row.
//
//   args.members.where(m => m.role == "admin" && m.active).count()
//   nodes.where(n => n.status == "active").orderByDesc(n => n.createdAt).first()
//
// Surface (all over a list receiver): where / select / first / last / single /
// any / all / count / sum / min / max / avg / orderBy / orderByDesc / take /
// skip / distinct / groupBy / reduce / empty / nodes. Membership is `v in list`.

/// Count the active admins among the passed members.
logic activeAdminCount {
  args {
    members []object!
  }
  return args.members.where(m => m.role == "admin" && m.active).count()
}

// Rules:
//   - Lambda bodies must be PURE -- a mutation / action call inside a lambda
//     is a load error (ADR §2.2). Reading bare `now` is fine.
//   - A query's value is its rows; a row reads its payload fields by name, and
//     the lowercase accessors read the list: `existing.empty()`,
//     `rows.count()`, `rows.first().email`. The capitalized
//     `.First()/.Nodes()/.Len()/.Empty()/.Count()` are retired.
//
// =============================================================================
//   7. Ambient vs imported (the clock and other nondeterminism) (ADR §2.1)
// =============================================================================
//
// Bare `now` is the AMBIENT current-timestamp primitive -- no import, captured
// at eval start (the `now()` / `timestamp()` call-forms are retired). A
// source of nondeterminism that reads fresh state at call time (a fresh
// wall-clock read, randomness) is an IMPORTED `core` dependency, so the
// file-top `use` block reveals it:
//
//   use core.{ timestamp }   // fresh wall-clock read -> declared dependency
//
// The full ambient-vs-imported classification + the whole construct matrix
// live in the durable record: docs/internal/design/core-builtins-and-collections-adr.md
// (§2.1 the purity line, §2.2 collections + temporal, §3 the construct matrix).
//
// Temporal reads are query-only: a query may carry `asOf args.at ?? latest`;
// a logic or an automation never time-travels directly (`asOf` in either is
// refused at parse, ADR §2.3 / #2305).
//
// =============================================================================
// See `_automation.memql` for the construct that legitimately writes, acts,
// publishes and reacts; and `_action.memql` for the external-capability
// construct.
// =============================================================================
